---
title: Scales, Guides, and Color
description: Reference for injected positional scales, automatic axes and margins, color scales, legends, themes, and gradients.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a compatible scale factory when its domain should come from mark channels.\nPass a scale instance when the domain is fixed application state. TanStack\nCharts copies the resolved scale, assigns its responsive pixel range, and uses\nthat copy for marks, ticks, and interaction. A supplied instance is never\nmutated."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put positional mappings in the chart's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales"},{"type":"text","value":" registry. The reserved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" entries are the default bindings for Cartesian marks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const scales = {\n  x: { scale: scaleBand, axis: { label: 'Product' } },\n  y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Revenue' } },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with the exact compact scale entry for numeric linear, band, point, or\nordinal mappings. Upgrade one mapping to D3 only when it needs temporal,\nnonlinear, radial, interpolated, or statistical scale semantics. The\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide owns that decision and direct\ndependency guidance. This page documents the TanStack Charts contract around\nboth implementations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"default-compact-scale-entries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default compact scale entries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts"},{"type":"text","value":" supplies four exact, tree-shakeable scale entries:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Entry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime export"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type contract"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/linear"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleLinear"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LinearScale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/band"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleBand"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BandScale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/point"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scalePoint"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PointScale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleOrdinal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"OrdinalScale"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LinearScale"},{"type":"text","value":" supports numeric two-stop domains and ranges, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clamp"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ticks"},{"type":"text","value":", basic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tickFormat"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"copy"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BandScale"},{"type":"text","value":" supports\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"padding"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paddingInner"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paddingOuter"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"align"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"round"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rangeRound"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandwidth"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"step"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"copy"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PointScale"},{"type":"text","value":" exposes the corresponding point\noperations with zero bandwidth. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"OrdinalScale"},{"type":"text","value":" supports explicit or implicit\ndomains, cyclic ranges, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"copy"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The factories and returned instances satisfy "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScaleInput"},{"type":"text","value":" directly. They\nare a documented subset, not a complete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" compatibility claim. Use D3\nfor temporal or transformed domains, piecewise and nonnumeric interpolation,\nand full D3 formatting semantics."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ConfiguredScaleLike"},{"type":"text","value":" may expose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invert(position)"},{"type":"text","value":". Charts copies that\ncapability onto "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ResolvedScale.invert"},{"type":"text","value":" after assigning the responsive range.\nFinal-screen layouts and interactions can then recover semantic values without\ncopying the authored scale. Band scales do not expose inversion."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"positional-scale-factories","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Positional scale factories"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The common path passes a compact factory directly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.16),\n  axis: { label: 'Product' },\n}\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  nice: true,\n  grid: true,\n  axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The chart creates a fresh scale for each layout, derives its domain from every\nmaterialized channel bound to that axis, applies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice"},{"type":"text","value":", and assigns the\nresponsive range. Bar and area baselines contribute zero when their baseline\nis implicit. Empty channels retain the factory's native domain."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return a configured scale from a zero-argument factory for options that should\nbe applied before domain inference:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.2),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice"},{"type":"text","value":" is an axis option because it must run after the inferred domain exists."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"d3-scale-upgrades","children":[{"type":"text","value":"D3 scale upgrades"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compact and D3 scales can coexist in one chart. This definition upgrades only\nits temporal x mapping:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true }\nconst y = { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true }","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleUtc"},{"type":"text","value":" when dates need elapsed-time spacing or\ncalendar-aware ticks. Compact band and point scales accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"},{"type":"text","value":" categories,\nbut adjacent categories remain equally spaced regardless of the time between\nthem."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other D3-only scale families cover logarithmic, power, symlog, square-root,\nradial, sequential, diverging, quantile, quantize, and threshold mappings. D3\nlinear scales add piecewise domains and ranges, nonnumeric interpolation, and\ncustom interpolators. The factory-versus-instance and responsive-range rules\nremain unchanged after an upgrade."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"fixed-domains","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed domains"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a scale instance when the domain is semantic application state:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Instances retain:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"their semantic domain"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"continuous, temporal, logarithmic, ordinal, or band mapping behavior"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tick generation and default tick formatting"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"clamping, unknown values, interpolation, and padding configured by the caller"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts owns:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"factory-domain inference"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the responsive pixel range"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"y-range orientation and optional axis reversal"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"centering values within a band"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"guide placement, label measurement, and margins"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scale copies make one configured scale safe to reuse across responsive scenes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"axis-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis options"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartPositionScaleOptions<\n  TValue extends ChartValue,\n> extends ChartAxisOptions<TValue> {\n  channel?: 'x' | 'y'\n  side?: 'top' | 'right' | 'bottom' | 'left'\n}\n\ninterface ChartGuideLineStyle {\n  stroke?: string\n  strokeOpacity?: number\n  strokeWidth?: number\n  strokeDasharray?: string\n  lineCap?: 'butt' | 'round' | 'square'\n}\n\ninterface ChartAxisOptions<TValue extends ChartValue> {\n  scale: ChartScale | ChartScaleInput<TValue>\n  nice?: boolean | number\n  reverse?: boolean\n  viewport?: ChartAxisViewportOptions<Extract<TValue, ChartContinuousValue>>\n  grid?: boolean | ChartGuideLineStyle\n  axis?:\n    | false\n    | {\n        line?: boolean | ChartGuideLineStyle\n        ticks?:\n          | false\n          | {\n              count?: number\n              spacing?: number\n              values?: readonly TValue[]\n              size?: number\n              line?: false | ChartGuideLineStyle\n              padding?: number\n              format?: (value: TValue) => string\n            }\n        tickLabels?:\n          | false\n          | {\n              rotate?: number\n              fontSize?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              fontWeight?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              opacity?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelOpacityContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              anchor?:\n                | 'start'\n                | 'middle'\n                | 'end'\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => 'start' | 'middle' | 'end' | undefined)\n              dx?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              dy?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              thin?:\n                | boolean\n                | {\n                    minGap?: number\n                    priority?: 'ends'\n                    keep?: readonly TValue[]\n                  }\n            }\n        label?:\n          | string\n          | {\n              text: string\n              offset?: number | 'auto'\n              fontSize?: number\n              fontWeight?: number\n              fill?: string\n              opacity?: number\n              motion?: ChartMotionDefinition\n            }\n      }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scale"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compact or D3 factory, configured instance, or custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nice the resolved domain using the responsive or supplied tick count."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reverse"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reverses the responsive pixel range without changing the caller's scale."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewport"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Commits a continuous semantic window and optional transient translation."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grid"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Draws grid rules from semantic tick candidates."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inferred axis"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis line, tick candidates, labels, and title; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" hides the axis."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"channel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inferred for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required on named scales; selects the Cartesian channel and range."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"side"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom"},{"type":"text","value":" for x; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"left"},{"type":"text","value":" for y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Places an x axis on top/bottom or a y axis on left/right."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grid"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.line"},{"type":"text","value":" to a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartGuideLineStyle"},{"type":"text","value":" object to enable the\nguide and override its stroke, opacity, width, dash pattern, or line cap.\nOmitted style fields keep the normal theme defaults. An empty object is\nequivalent to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.line"},{"type":"text","value":" styles only the axis baseline, not its\ntick stubs. Use finite non-negative widths and opacity values from zero to one."}]},{"type":"code","value":"type ChartContinuousValue = number | Date\n\ntype ChartContinuousDomain<\n  TValue extends ChartContinuousValue = ChartContinuousValue,\n> =\n  | (Extract<TValue, number> extends never ? never : readonly [number, number])\n  | (Extract<TValue, Date> extends never ? never : readonly [Date, Date])\n\ninterface ChartAxisViewportOptions<\n  TValue extends ChartContinuousValue = ChartContinuousValue,\n> {\n  domain: ChartContinuousDomain<TValue>\n  translate?: number\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartContinuousDomain"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps numeric and temporal endpoints homogeneous at\nthe type boundary. At runtime, a viewport requires two distinct finite values\nand a configured or inferable continuous scale with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invert"},{"type":"text","value":", ticks, clamping\ndisabled, and independently configurable domain and range behavior. Band,\nordinal, quantize, clamped, and getter-only scale inputs are rejected. An\nauthored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.viewport"},{"type":"text","value":" is also rejected for a custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":".\nLogarithmic content and viewport domains must be finite, nonzero, and stay on\nthe same side of zero."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"translate"},{"type":"text","value":" is applied in screen-direction scene pixels after semantic\nmapping: positive x moves right and positive y moves down, regardless of\ndomain order or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reverse"},{"type":"text","value":". Guides remain fixed. Viewport-dependent marks are\nclipped and translated per mark and per owned axis; see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md#continuous-viewports","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Continuous viewports"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales.x"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales.y"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" only when no mark uses that positional\nscale. To keep the scale while hiding its axis, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis: false"},{"type":"text","value":". Grid\nvisibility remains independent."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without an explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks"},{"type":"text","value":" policy, the responsive target is\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clamp(2, floor(chart.width / 92), 8)"},{"type":"text","value":" for x and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clamp(2, floor(chart.height / 48), 7)"},{"type":"text","value":" for y. The configured scale may return\na different number of ticks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice: true"},{"type":"text","value":" uses that responsive or authored tick count when rounding a scale\ndomain, even if the axis is hidden. Use a number such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice: 5"},{"type":"text","value":" when a small\nsparkline needs domain rounding independent of its size."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks: false"},{"type":"text","value":" removes both tick stubs and their candidate labels. To keep\nlabels while hiding only the stubs, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis: { ticks: { size: 0 } }"},{"type":"text","value":". The\nbaseline is separate, hide it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.line: false"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks.line"},{"type":"text","value":" to style the stubs with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartGuideLineStyle"},{"type":"text","value":", or set\nit to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" to hide just the stubs. This option does not change the\nbaseline, grid, tick values, or label formatting."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const axis = {\n  line: { stroke: 'navy' },\n  ticks: { line: { stroke: 'navy', strokeWidth: 2 } },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"count"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spacing"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":" are mutually exclusive candidate policies.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"count"},{"type":"text","value":" is a scale hint, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spacing"},{"type":"text","value":" derives that hint from the final axis length,\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":" supplies exact semantic candidates. Grid lines and tick stubs use\nthese candidates before label thinning."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick labels are horizontal and collision-thinned by default. Rotation is\nexplicit and independent:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: scaleUtc,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: { spacing: 80, size: 0 },\n    tickLabels: {\n      rotate: -35,\n      thin: {\n        minGap: 8,\n        priority: 'ends',\n        keep: [launchDate],\n      },\n    },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"thin: false"},{"type":"text","value":" renders every candidate label. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keep"},{"type":"text","value":" is a hard guarantee:\nkept values render even if they collide with one another. A kept value outside\nthe candidate set adds only a label, not a tick stub or grid line."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick-label presentation accepts constants or accessors. Accessors run on every\ncandidate before thinning and receive the semantic value, stable candidate\nindex, resolved center position, and resolved band width. Continuous scales\nreport a bandwidth of zero. Returning "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" preserves the normal value\nfor that candidate."}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue extends ChartValue> {\n  value: TValue\n  index: number\n  position: number\n  bandwidth: number\n}\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: scaleBand<number>().domain(weeks),\n  axis: {\n    tickLabels: {\n      fontSize: 13,\n      opacity: 0.62,\n      anchor: ({ index }) => (index === 0 ? 'start' : undefined),\n      dx: ({ index, bandwidth }) => (index === 0 ? -bandwidth / 2 : undefined),\n    },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to an outward anchor derived from x rotation or the y-axis\nside. The automatic value accounts for the host's inline direction. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dx"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dy"},{"type":"text","value":" apply after the normal tick position and padding. Resolved font size,\nweight, anchor, offset, opacity, and rotation all participate in collision\nthinning and automatic margins. Numeric typography follows tick-label motion;\nanchor changes snap."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An opacity callback also receives "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer"},{"type":"text","value":", both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" during\ninitial layout. It runs again when focus changes, updating paint without\nrebuilding marks, measuring text, or changing tick placement and thinning.\nClearing focus restores the initial opacity. Other tick-label callbacks remain\nlayout-only. For example, hide the tick that matches the focused x value:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const tickLabels = {\n  opacity: ({ value, focus }) =>\n    focus && Object.is(value, focus.primary.xValue) ? 0 : 1,\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis titles keep the compact string form when only text is needed. Use the\nobject form for title typography, paint, offset, or motion:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  axis: {\n    label: {\n      text: 'Average order value (PLN)',\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fontWeight: 500,\n      fill: '#363636',\n      opacity: 0.9,\n    },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" sets the text color. Omitted presentation fields retain the existing\ntitle defaults: responsive 10 or 11 pixel sizing on x, 11 pixels on y, weight\n600, theme foreground fill, and 0.76 fill opacity. Configured font size and\nweight participate in text measurement, automatic offsets, and automatic\nmargins for SVG, Canvas, and native rendering."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"named-scales-and-multiple-axes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Named scales and multiple axes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reserved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" entries are the default bindings for Cartesian marks.\nAdd another entry when a mark needs an independent mapping, then bind that mark\nwith "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xScale"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yScale"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(revenue, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    lineY(conversion, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'rate',\n      yScale: 'conversion',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n    },\n    conversion: {\n      channel: 'y',\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      side: 'right',\n      axis: {\n        label: 'Conversion',\n        ticks: { format: (value: number) => `${Math.round(value * 100)}%` },\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"70-composed-chart","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A named scale must declare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"channel: 'x'"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"channel: 'y'"},{"type":"text","value":". Its mark binding\nmust use the same channel. The ID "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" is reserved for the shared visual\ncolor scale and cannot name a Cartesian position scale."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every non-null scale renders an axis by default. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis: false"},{"type":"text","value":" when a\nmapping should not draw another axis. X scales can use the top or bottom side,\nand y scales can use the left or right side. Axes on the same side stack\noutward and contribute their measured size to the automatic margin."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis sides stay physical in a right-to-left container, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"side: 'right'"},{"type":"text","value":"\nstill puts the axis on the right. Automatic tick anchors account for the\ncontainer's inline direction and keep labels outside the plot. An explicitly\nauthored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" anchor remains logical. Text measurement, SVG,\nCanvas, native rendering, and standalone SVG export preserve that direction."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-guide-layout","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic guide layout"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a margin side is omitted, the scene compiler measures:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"formatted tick glyph bounds"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tick rotation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"first and last label overhang"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"axis title bounds and offset"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cartesian "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":"-mark anchors, pixel offsets, and rotation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"color legend height"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The DOM host measures the inherited container font and relayouts after web\nfonts load. Static compilation uses deterministic estimates unless\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"measureText"},{"type":"text","value":" is supplied. Explicit margin sides remain locked, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clip: true"},{"type":"text","value":" prevents clipped mark labels from expanding the plot."}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartTextMeasurer {\n  (\n    text: string,\n    options: {\n      fontSize: number\n      fontWeight?: number\n      fontFamily: string\n      fontStyle: string\n      fontStretch: string\n      letterSpacing: number\n      direction: 'ltr' | 'rtl' | 'inherit'\n      locale?: string\n      fontScale: number\n      anchor: 'start' | 'middle' | 'end'\n      baseline: 'auto' | 'middle' | 'hanging'\n    },\n  ): {\n    x: number\n    y: number\n    width: number\n    height: number\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The returned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" locate the painted glyph box relative to the requested\nanchor and baseline. Pass the measurer through a host, adapter, runtime render,\nor "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartScene"},{"type":"text","value":" layout options. The callback is synchronous; a host owns\nfont loading or asynchronous native measurement and recompiles the scene when\nthose metrics become ready. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fontScale"},{"type":"text","value":" applies to the painted font size and\nletter spacing, so custom measurers must include it in their result."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom scales"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":" is the final extension boundary for a nonstandard positional\nmapping. Prefer a compact scale, then a D3 scale with the required semantics,\nbefore implementing this contract:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartScale {\n  id: string\n  resolve(context: ChartScaleResolveContext): ResolvedScale\n}\n\ntype ChartScaleResolver = (context: ChartScaleResolveContext) => ResolvedScale","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The resolver context contains "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", all materialized "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":", the responsive\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"range"},{"type":"text","value":", the axis "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options"},{"type":"text","value":", a target "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tickCount"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeZero"},{"type":"text","value":". It must\nreturn a complete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ResolvedScale"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ResolvedScale {\n  id: string\n  type: string\n  domain: readonly ChartValue[]\n  map(value: unknown): number\n  invert?(position: number): ChartValue\n  ticks: readonly { value: ChartValue; label: string; position: number }[]\n  bandwidth: number\n  viewport?: {\n    contentDomain: readonly ChartValue[]\n    domain: ChartContinuousDomain\n    translate: number\n    map(value: unknown): number\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer a compact or D3 scale when it can express the mapping. A custom scale\nowns correct domains, finite mapping, ticks, formatting, bandwidth, and\nresponse to the supplied range. Do not wrap an existing compact or D3 scale in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host cannot apply an authored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.viewport"},{"type":"text","value":" to an opaque custom\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":". A custom resolver can return a complete\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ResolvedScale.viewport"},{"type":"text","value":" itself, in which case it owns the content domain,\ncommitted domain, translation, and presented mapper."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"color","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Color"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data-paint marks accept a semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel. On marks that also expose\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":", grouping remains independent and supplies the color value only when\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"},{"type":"text","value":" are final paint overrides, so they do\nnot contribute to the scale or legend."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Omit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color.scale"},{"type":"text","value":" to use the theme palette. Use the compact ordinal scale when\ncategories need a stable application-owned mapping:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst statusColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['healthy', 'warning', 'critical'],\n  ['#16a34a', '#f59e0b', '#dc2626'],\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upgrade color to D3 for continuous interpolation or quantile, quantize, and\nthreshold policy."}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ConfiguredColorScaleLike<TValue extends ChartKey, TOutput> {\n  (value: TValue): TOutput\n  copy: () => ConfiguredColorScaleLike<TValue, TOutput>\n  domain?: () => readonly TValue[]\n  range?: () => readonly TOutput[]\n}\n\ninterface ChartColorOptions {\n  scale?: ConfiguredColorScaleLike<any, any> | ChartColorScaleFactory<any, any>\n  resolver?: ChartColorScale\n  domain?: readonly ChartKey[]\n  range?: readonly string[]\n  nice?: boolean | number\n  legend?: ChartColorLegend\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scale"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compatible factory with inference or instance with a fixed domain"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolver"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom color-scale resolver"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"domain"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observed channel values"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Domain hint for factory, built-in, or custom resolution"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"range"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See below"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Range for a factory, the built-in scale, or a custom resolver"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nice a factory or configured continuous color scale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"legend"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legend layout and scene renderer shown above the inner chart"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolution order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color.scale"},{"type":"text","value":", created or copied before use"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color.resolver"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the built-in ordinal scale using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"domain"},{"type":"text","value":" or observed channel values and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"range"},{"type":"text","value":" or the theme palette"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A color-scale factory is classified by its capabilities:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ordinal factories infer first-seen distinct values;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"continuous and quantize factories infer the finite extent;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"quantile factories receive the complete numeric population;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"threshold factories require an explicit domain of authored cuts."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The theme palette is the range default only for the built-in ordinal scale.\nA D3 factory must receive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color.range"},{"type":"text","value":" or return a scale already configured\nwith a non-empty string range or interpolator; bare D3 factories retain\nnumeric or empty defaults that are not valid paint. Multi-stop continuous\nranges receive evenly spaced domain stops across the extent. A supplied scale\ninstance retains its domain and range. Outputs are converted to strings. A\ncustom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartColorScale"},{"type":"text","value":" receives observed values, optional domain and range,\nand the resolved theme, then returns:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ResolvedColorScale {\n  type: string\n  kind?: 'categorical' | 'continuous' | 'quantile' | 'quantize' | 'threshold'\n  domain: readonly ChartKey[]\n  range: readonly string[]\n  thresholds?: readonly number[]\n  map(value: ChartKey | null | undefined): string\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartKey"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string | number"},{"type":"text","value":". On the built-in and configured-scale paths, a\nnull color value maps to the first range color or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"currentColor"},{"type":"text","value":". A custom\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartColorScale"},{"type":"text","value":" owns its null mapping through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ResolvedColorScale.map"},{"type":"text","value":". A\ncustom stepped scale supplies exact interior legend boundaries with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"thresholds"},{"type":"text","value":"; D3 quantile, quantize, and threshold scales derive them\nautomatically."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-color-legend","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic color legend"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { colorLegend, colorLegendItems } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  label: 'Package',\n  itemWidth: 120,\n  items: colorLegendItems({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 16,\n    indicator: { shape: 'square' },\n    label: { fontSize: 12 },\n  }),\n  width: 240,\n  format: (value) => value.toFixed(0),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"interface ColorLegendOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  label?: string\n  itemWidth?: number\n  items?: ColorLegendItems<TValue>\n  width?: number\n  format?: (value: number) => string\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n}\n\ndeclare function colorLegendItems<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey>(\n  options?: ColorLegendItemOptions<TValue>,\n): ColorLegendItems<TValue>\n\n// Opaque presentation returned by colorLegendItems().\ninterface ColorLegendItems<in TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {}\n\ninterface ColorLegendItemContext {\n  color: string\n  index: number\n  label: string\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendIndicatorRenderContext extends ColorLegendItemContext {\n  bounds: ChartBounds\n}\n\ntype ColorLegendItemValue<TValue extends ChartKey, TResult> =\n  TResult | ((value: TValue, context: ColorLegendItemContext) => TResult)\n\ntype ColorLegendIndicatorShape = 'dot' | 'square' | 'line' | 'line-dot'\n\ninterface ColorLegendItemOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  justify?: 'start' | 'center' | 'stretch'\n  gap?: number\n  rowGap?: number\n  indicator?: ColorLegendIndicatorOptions<TValue>\n  label?: ColorLegendLabelOptions<TValue>\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendIndicatorOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  shape?: ColorLegendItemValue<TValue, ColorLegendIndicatorShape>\n  width?: number\n  height?: number\n  gap?: number\n  render?: (\n    value: TValue,\n    context: ColorLegendIndicatorRenderContext,\n  ) => SceneNode | readonly SceneNode[]\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendLabelOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  format?: (value: TValue) => string\n  fontSize?: number\n  fontWeight?: number\n  fill?: ColorLegendItemValue<TValue, string>\n  fillOpacity?: number\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":" applies only to categorical scales. The default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stretch"},{"type":"text","value":"\njustification uses equal-width columns. In this mode, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"itemWidth"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"110"},{"type":"text","value":" and is clamped to a minimum of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"64"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"center"},{"type":"text","value":" instead measure\neach formatted label with the chart host's configured typography, wrap compact\nrows, and use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gap"},{"type":"text","value":" between items. Invalid host measurements fall back to the\nsame deterministic estimator used by chart layout."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configured items default to a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"16"},{"type":"text","value":" pixel compact-layout gap, an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8"},{"type":"text","value":" pixel row\ngap, an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8"},{"type":"text","value":" by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8"},{"type":"text","value":" dot with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"5"},{"type":"text","value":" pixel label gap, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"11"},{"type":"text","value":" pixel label text, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"String"},{"type":"text","value":" label formatting. Measured label height reserves enough room for each\nrow. If an indicator is wider than its allocated item, its bounds stop at the\nitem edge."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"indicator.shape"},{"type":"text","value":" selects a dot, square, line, or line with an outlined point.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"height"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gap"},{"type":"text","value":" reserve the indicator box and its distance from\nthe label. A custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":" callback receives the categorical value plus\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ bounds, color, index, label }"},{"type":"text","value":"; the built-in legend still measures, wraps,\nand positions the item. Label "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks receive the same resolved color\nand formatted label. A line-dot center uses the chart background, or the host's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Canvas"},{"type":"text","value":" color when that background is transparent, so it remains hollow on\nlight and dark hosts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An item presentation can handle the same value type as its legend or a broader\none. TypeScript rejects a narrower presentation because its callbacks could\notherwise receive a value they do not accept."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Continuous scales render a sampled ramp. Quantize, quantile, and threshold\nscales render exact range bins at their resolved thresholds. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":" and the\ntop-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":" configure these quantitative forms, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":" is ignored.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placement"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This mixed-series legend uses only the resolved color scale for labels and\npaint:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type Series = 'Revenue' | 'Orders'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  items: colorLegendItems<Series>({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 20,\n    rowGap: 10,\n    indicator: {\n      width: 20,\n      height: 14,\n      gap: 6,\n      shape: (series) => (series === 'Revenue' ? 'line-dot' : 'square'),\n    },\n    label: {\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fill: (_series, { color }) => color,\n    },\n  }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"gradient-legend","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gradient legend"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { colorGradientLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ncolorGradientLegend({\n  label: 'Density',\n  steps: 48,\n  width: 240,\n  format: (value) => value.toFixed(1),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"interface ColorGradientLegendOptions {\n  label?: string\n  steps?: number\n  width?: number\n  format?: (value: number) => string\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"steps"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"32"},{"type":"text","value":" and is clamped to at least "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"240"},{"type":"text","value":", uses at least "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"80"},{"type":"text","value":" when space permits, and is finally capped by the\ninner chart width. The legend requires a numeric first and last color-domain\nvalue and throws for a nonnumeric domain."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"interactive-categorical-legend","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactive categorical legend"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ninteractiveColorLegend({\n  visible: controlledSignal(visibleSeries, setVisibleSeries),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  ariaLabel: 'Series visibility',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"interface InteractiveColorLegendChange<TValue extends ChartKey> {\n  type: 'toggle'\n  value: TValue\n  visible: boolean\n}\n\ninterface InteractiveColorLegendItemContext {\n  visible: boolean\n}\n\ninterface InteractiveColorLegendOptions<TValue extends ChartKey> {\n  hover?: 'series' | false\n  visible: ControlledSignal<\n    readonly TValue[],\n    InteractiveColorLegendChange<TValue>\n  >\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n  ariaLabel?: string\n  itemWidth?: number\n  format?: (value: TValue) => string\n  itemAriaLabel?: (\n    value: TValue,\n    context: InteractiveColorLegendItemContext,\n  ) => string\n  emptyLabel?: string\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The application owns the current visible-value snapshot and handles each\nproposed replacement. The legend owns domain-ordered toggling, responsive\nlayout, and native browser buttons. It filters series geometry and focus points\nafter scale resolution, so hidden values remain in categorical and positional\ndomains. A mark participates when its categorical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel establishes\nseries identity without a separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" channel. Dot marks also participate when\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" supplies their series and default color, with no separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The DOM hosts replace the scene fallback with native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"button"},{"type":"text","value":" elements. Static\nSVG output retains a noninteractive visual fallback. This control is not yet\nimplemented by the React Native host."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hover: 'series'"},{"type":"text","value":" to match inline mark states against the series under the\npointer or on the focused legend button. Its state context has "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source: 'legend'"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"matches('series')"},{"type":"text","value":" identifies the emphasized category. Leaving\nor blurring restores the chart's interaction styling. Hidden series do not\nreceive emphasis."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Emphasis matches semantic series, not rendered color strings. Line and area\nmarks can establish a series through categorical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":". For dot marks, use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z: 'series'"},{"type":"text","value":" to establish series ownership; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" also supplies the default\ncolor channel, so a second "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color: 'series'"},{"type":"text","value":" is unnecessary. A color-only dot\ncan vary its paint while remaining outside any semantic series."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legend emphasis does not set interaction focus, show a tooltip, move a\ncrosshair, or activate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused"},{"type":"text","value":" geometry. Existing pinned focus and its\ntooltip stay in place. Tick-label opacity still sees the real interaction\nfocus. Static SVG remains noninteractive. A custom renderer must declare\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"supportsStateFocus: true"},{"type":"text","value":" and honor the fourth "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paintFocus"},{"type":"text","value":" argument's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stateFocus"},{"type":"text","value":", otherwise requesting emphasis throws."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-legends","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom legends"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartColorLegend"},{"type":"text","value":" separates layout from rendering:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartColorLegend {\n  height(itemCount: number, context: ChartColorLegendContext): number\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n  render(context: ChartColorLegendContext): SceneNode\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"height"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the reserved pixel height before chart bounds are finalized.\nBoth callbacks receive the resolved colors, plot and legend bounds, theme, and\nfull chart size. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":" returns one keyed\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./runtime-and-scene.md#scene-nodes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"scene node"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Browser host controls are an\nadvanced extension boundary used by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interactiveColorLegend"},{"type":"text","value":"; ordinary custom\nlegends should remain renderer-neutral scene output."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"theme-and-gradients","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Theme and gradients"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The built-in theme is described in "},{"type":"link","href":"./chart-spec.md#theme","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart spec"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\nChart gradients are renderer-neutral resources:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartGradientBase {\n  id: string\n  stops: readonly ChartGradientStop[]\n}\n\ninterface ChartGradientStop {\n  offset: number\n  color: string\n  opacity?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartLinearGradient extends ChartGradientBase {\n  type?: 'linear'\n  x1?: number\n  y1?: number\n  x2?: number\n  y2?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartRadialGradient extends ChartGradientBase {\n  type: 'radial'\n  cx?: number\n  cy?: number\n  r?: number\n  fx?: number\n  fy?: number\n}\n\ntype ChartGradient = ChartLinearGradient | ChartRadialGradient","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coordinates and stop offsets use SVG-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"objectBoundingBox"},{"type":"text","value":" values\nnormalized from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":". Renderers clamp values to that range. Stops keep\ntheir authored order, and an offset below the previous stop is clamped forward\nto the previous offset. A linear gradient may omit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type"},{"type":"text","value":" for compatibility.\nIts omitted coordinates form a vertical gradient from bottom to top, from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(0, 1)"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(0, 0)"},{"type":"text","value":". A radial gradient requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type: 'radial'"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cx"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cy"},{"type":"text","value":",\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"r"},{"type":"text","value":" default to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0.5"},{"type":"text","value":", while omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fx"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fy"},{"type":"text","value":" independently inherit the\nresolved center coordinate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reference either kind from a mark's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"},{"type":"text","value":" as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"url(#resource-id)"},{"type":"text","value":".\nSVG and React Native support both kinds for fills and strokes. Canvas renders\nexact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"objectBoundingBox"},{"type":"text","value":" radial fills, including the elliptical result on a\nnon-square shape. Canvas radial strokes are not supported because a nonuniform\nbounds transform would also distort stroke width; the Canvas renderer throws\ninstead of painting a misleading approximation. Linear gradients continue to\nsupport Canvas fills and strokes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartGradient"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartGradientBase"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartGradientStop"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartLinearGradient"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartRadialGradient"},{"type":"text","value":" are type exports from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/universal"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/types"},{"type":"text","value":". Use an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idPrefix"},{"type":"text","value":" when charts share a document; see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./rendering-and-export.md#svg-resources","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rendering and export"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"default-compact-scale-entries","text":"Default compact scale entries","level":2},{"id":"positional-scale-factories","text":"Positional scale factories","level":2},{"id":"d3-scale-upgrades","text":"D3 scale upgrades","level":2},{"id":"fixed-domains","text":"Fixed domains","level":2},{"id":"axis-options","text":"Axis options","level":2},{"id":"named-scales-and-multiple-axes","text":"Named scales and multiple axes","level":2},{"id":"automatic-guide-layout","text":"Automatic guide layout","level":2},{"id":"custom-scales","text":"Custom scales","level":2},{"id":"color","text":"Color","level":2},{"id":"automatic-color-legend","text":"Automatic color legend","level":2},{"id":"gradient-legend","text":"Gradient legend","level":2},{"id":"interactive-categorical-legend","text":"Interactive categorical legend","level":2},{"id":"custom-legends","text":"Custom legends","level":2},{"id":"theme-and-gradients","text":"Theme and gradients","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nPass a compatible scale factory when its domain should come from mark channels.\nPass a scale instance when the domain is fixed application state. TanStack\nCharts copies the resolved scale, assigns its responsive pixel range, and uses\nthat copy for marks, ticks, and interaction. A supplied instance is never\nmutated.\n\nPut positional mappings in the chart's `scales` registry. The reserved `x`\nand `y` entries are the default bindings for Cartesian marks:\n\n```ts\nconst scales = {\n  x: { scale: scaleBand, axis: { label: 'Product' } },\n  y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Revenue' } },\n}\n```\n\nStart with the exact compact scale entry for numeric linear, band, point, or\nordinal mappings. Upgrade one mapping to D3 only when it needs temporal,\nnonlinear, radial, interpolated, or statistical scale semantics. The\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) guide owns that decision and direct\ndependency guidance. This page documents the TanStack Charts contract around\nboth implementations.\n\n## Default compact scale entries\n\n`@tanstack/charts` supplies four exact, tree-shakeable scale entries:\n\n| Entry                             | Runtime export | Type contract  |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------- |\n| `@tanstack/charts/scales/linear`  | `scaleLinear`  | `LinearScale`  |\n| `@tanstack/charts/scales/band`    | `scaleBand`    | `BandScale`    |\n| `@tanstack/charts/scales/point`   | `scalePoint`   | `PointScale`   |\n| `@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal` | `scaleOrdinal` | `OrdinalScale` |\n\n`LinearScale` supports numeric two-stop domains and ranges, `invert`, `clamp`,\n`ticks`, basic `tickFormat`, `nice`, and `copy`. `BandScale` supports\n`padding`, `paddingInner`, `paddingOuter`, `align`, `round`, `rangeRound`,\n`bandwidth`, `step`, and `copy`. `PointScale` exposes the corresponding point\noperations with zero bandwidth. `OrdinalScale` supports explicit or implicit\ndomains, cyclic ranges, `unknown`, and `copy`.\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]),\n}\n```\n\nThe factories and returned instances satisfy `ChartScaleInput` directly. They\nare a documented subset, not a complete `d3-scale` compatibility claim. Use D3\nfor temporal or transformed domains, piecewise and nonnumeric interpolation,\nand full D3 formatting semantics.\n\n`ConfiguredScaleLike` may expose `invert(position)`. Charts copies that\ncapability onto `ResolvedScale.invert` after assigning the responsive range.\nFinal-screen layouts and interactions can then recover semantic values without\ncopying the authored scale. Band scales do not expose inversion.\n\n## Positional scale factories\n\nThe common path passes a compact factory directly:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.16),\n  axis: { label: 'Product' },\n}\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  nice: true,\n  grid: true,\n  axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n}\n```\n\nThe chart creates a fresh scale for each layout, derives its domain from every\nmaterialized channel bound to that axis, applies `nice`, and assigns the\nresponsive range. Bar and area baselines contribute zero when their baseline\nis implicit. Empty channels retain the factory's native domain.\n\nReturn a configured scale from a zero-argument factory for options that should\nbe applied before domain inference:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.2),\n}\n```\n\n`nice` is an axis option because it must run after the inferred domain exists.\n\n## D3 scale upgrades\n\nCompact and D3 scales can coexist in one chart. This definition upgrades only\nits temporal x mapping:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true }\nconst y = { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true }\n```\n\nUse `scaleTime` or `scaleUtc` when dates need elapsed-time spacing or\ncalendar-aware ticks. Compact band and point scales accept `Date` categories,\nbut adjacent categories remain equally spaced regardless of the time between\nthem.\n\nOther D3-only scale families cover logarithmic, power, symlog, square-root,\nradial, sequential, diverging, quantile, quantize, and threshold mappings. D3\nlinear scales add piecewise domains and ranges, nonnumeric interpolation, and\ncustom interpolators. The factory-versus-instance and responsive-range rules\nremain unchanged after an upgrade.\n\n## Fixed domains\n\nPass a scale instance when the domain is semantic application state:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]),\n}\n```\n\nInstances retain:\n\n- their semantic domain\n- continuous, temporal, logarithmic, ordinal, or band mapping behavior\n- tick generation and default tick formatting\n- clamping, unknown values, interpolation, and padding configured by the caller\n\nTanStack Charts owns:\n\n- factory-domain inference\n- the responsive pixel range\n- y-range orientation and optional axis reversal\n- centering values within a band\n- guide placement, label measurement, and margins\n\nScale copies make one configured scale safe to reuse across responsive scenes.\n\n## Axis options\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartPositionScaleOptions<\n  TValue extends ChartValue,\n> extends ChartAxisOptions<TValue> {\n  channel?: 'x' | 'y'\n  side?: 'top' | 'right' | 'bottom' | 'left'\n}\n\ninterface ChartGuideLineStyle {\n  stroke?: string\n  strokeOpacity?: number\n  strokeWidth?: number\n  strokeDasharray?: string\n  lineCap?: 'butt' | 'round' | 'square'\n}\n\ninterface ChartAxisOptions<TValue extends ChartValue> {\n  scale: ChartScale | ChartScaleInput<TValue>\n  nice?: boolean | number\n  reverse?: boolean\n  viewport?: ChartAxisViewportOptions<Extract<TValue, ChartContinuousValue>>\n  grid?: boolean | ChartGuideLineStyle\n  axis?:\n    | false\n    | {\n        line?: boolean | ChartGuideLineStyle\n        ticks?:\n          | false\n          | {\n              count?: number\n              spacing?: number\n              values?: readonly TValue[]\n              size?: number\n              line?: false | ChartGuideLineStyle\n              padding?: number\n              format?: (value: TValue) => string\n            }\n        tickLabels?:\n          | false\n          | {\n              rotate?: number\n              fontSize?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              fontWeight?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              opacity?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelOpacityContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              anchor?:\n                | 'start'\n                | 'middle'\n                | 'end'\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => 'start' | 'middle' | 'end' | undefined)\n              dx?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              dy?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              thin?:\n                | boolean\n                | {\n                    minGap?: number\n                    priority?: 'ends'\n                    keep?: readonly TValue[]\n                  }\n            }\n        label?:\n          | string\n          | {\n              text: string\n              offset?: number | 'auto'\n              fontSize?: number\n              fontWeight?: number\n              fill?: string\n              opacity?: number\n              motion?: ChartMotionDefinition\n            }\n      }\n}\n```\n\n| Option     | Default                      | Meaning                                                                  |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `scale`    | Required                     | Compact or D3 factory, configured instance, or custom `ChartScale`.      |\n| `nice`     | `false`                      | Nice the resolved domain using the responsive or supplied tick count.    |\n| `reverse`  | `false`                      | Reverses the responsive pixel range without changing the caller's scale. |\n| `viewport` | None                         | Commits a continuous semantic window and optional transient translation. |\n| `grid`     | `false`                      | Draws grid rules from semantic tick candidates.                          |\n| `axis`     | Inferred axis                | Axis line, tick candidates, labels, and title; `false` hides the axis.   |\n| `channel`  | Inferred for `x` and `y`     | Required on named scales; selects the Cartesian channel and range.       |\n| `side`     | `bottom` for x; `left` for y | Places an x axis on top/bottom or a y axis on left/right.                |\n\nSet `grid` or `axis.line` to a `ChartGuideLineStyle` object to enable the\nguide and override its stroke, opacity, width, dash pattern, or line cap.\nOmitted style fields keep the normal theme defaults. An empty object is\nequivalent to `true`, and `axis.line` styles only the axis baseline, not its\ntick stubs. Use finite non-negative widths and opacity values from zero to one.\n\n```ts\ntype ChartContinuousValue = number | Date\n\ntype ChartContinuousDomain<\n  TValue extends ChartContinuousValue = ChartContinuousValue,\n> =\n  | (Extract<TValue, number> extends never ? never : readonly [number, number])\n  | (Extract<TValue, Date> extends never ? never : readonly [Date, Date])\n\ninterface ChartAxisViewportOptions<\n  TValue extends ChartContinuousValue = ChartContinuousValue,\n> {\n  domain: ChartContinuousDomain<TValue>\n  translate?: number\n}\n```\n\n`ChartContinuousDomain` keeps numeric and temporal endpoints homogeneous at\nthe type boundary. At runtime, a viewport requires two distinct finite values\nand a configured or inferable continuous scale with `invert`, ticks, clamping\ndisabled, and independently configurable domain and range behavior. Band,\nordinal, quantize, clamped, and getter-only scale inputs are rejected. An\nauthored `axis.viewport` is also rejected for a custom `ChartScale`.\nLogarithmic content and viewport domains must be finite, nonzero, and stay on\nthe same side of zero.\n\n`translate` is applied in screen-direction scene pixels after semantic\nmapping: positive x moves right and positive y moves down, regardless of\ndomain order or `reverse`. Guides remain fixed. Viewport-dependent marks are\nclipped and translated per mark and per owned axis; see\n[Continuous viewports](../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md#continuous-viewports).\n\nSet `scales.x` or `scales.y` to `null` only when no mark uses that positional\nscale. To keep the scale while hiding its axis, use `axis: false`. Grid\nvisibility remains independent.\n\nWithout an explicit `axis.ticks` policy, the responsive target is\n`clamp(2, floor(chart.width / 92), 8)` for x and\n`clamp(2, floor(chart.height / 48), 7)` for y. The configured scale may return\na different number of ticks.\n\n`nice: true` uses that responsive or authored tick count when rounding a scale\ndomain, even if the axis is hidden. Use a number such as `nice: 5` when a small\nsparkline needs domain rounding independent of its size.\n\n`axis.ticks: false` removes both tick stubs and their candidate labels. To keep\nlabels while hiding only the stubs, use `axis: { ticks: { size: 0 } }`. The\nbaseline is separate, hide it with `axis.line: false`.\n\nUse `axis.ticks.line` to style the stubs with `ChartGuideLineStyle`, or set\nit to `false` to hide just the stubs. This option does not change the\nbaseline, grid, tick values, or label formatting.\n\n```ts\nconst axis = {\n  line: { stroke: 'navy' },\n  ticks: { line: { stroke: 'navy', strokeWidth: 2 } },\n}\n```\n\n`count`, `spacing`, and `values` are mutually exclusive candidate policies.\n`count` is a scale hint, `spacing` derives that hint from the final axis length,\nand `values` supplies exact semantic candidates. Grid lines and tick stubs use\nthese candidates before label thinning.\n\nTick labels are horizontal and collision-thinned by default. Rotation is\nexplicit and independent:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: scaleUtc,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: { spacing: 80, size: 0 },\n    tickLabels: {\n      rotate: -35,\n      thin: {\n        minGap: 8,\n        priority: 'ends',\n        keep: [launchDate],\n      },\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`thin: false` renders every candidate label. `keep` is a hard guarantee:\nkept values render even if they collide with one another. A kept value outside\nthe candidate set adds only a label, not a tick stub or grid line.\n\nTick-label presentation accepts constants or accessors. Accessors run on every\ncandidate before thinning and receive the semantic value, stable candidate\nindex, resolved center position, and resolved band width. Continuous scales\nreport a bandwidth of zero. Returning `undefined` preserves the normal value\nfor that candidate.\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue extends ChartValue> {\n  value: TValue\n  index: number\n  position: number\n  bandwidth: number\n}\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: scaleBand<number>().domain(weeks),\n  axis: {\n    tickLabels: {\n      fontSize: 13,\n      opacity: 0.62,\n      anchor: ({ index }) => (index === 0 ? 'start' : undefined),\n      dx: ({ index, bandwidth }) => (index === 0 ? -bandwidth / 2 : undefined),\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`anchor` defaults to an outward anchor derived from x rotation or the y-axis\nside. The automatic value accounts for the host's inline direction. `dx` and\n`dy` apply after the normal tick position and padding. Resolved font size,\nweight, anchor, offset, opacity, and rotation all participate in collision\nthinning and automatic margins. Numeric typography follows tick-label motion;\nanchor changes snap.\n\nAn opacity callback also receives `focus` and `pointer`, both `null` during\ninitial layout. It runs again when focus changes, updating paint without\nrebuilding marks, measuring text, or changing tick placement and thinning.\nClearing focus restores the initial opacity. Other tick-label callbacks remain\nlayout-only. For example, hide the tick that matches the focused x value:\n\n```ts\nconst tickLabels = {\n  opacity: ({ value, focus }) =>\n    focus && Object.is(value, focus.primary.xValue) ? 0 : 1,\n}\n```\n\nAxis titles keep the compact string form when only text is needed. Use the\nobject form for title typography, paint, offset, or motion:\n\n```ts\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  axis: {\n    label: {\n      text: 'Average order value (PLN)',\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fontWeight: 500,\n      fill: '#363636',\n      opacity: 0.9,\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`fill` sets the text color. Omitted presentation fields retain the existing\ntitle defaults: responsive 10 or 11 pixel sizing on x, 11 pixels on y, weight\n600, theme foreground fill, and 0.76 fill opacity. Configured font size and\nweight participate in text measurement, automatic offsets, and automatic\nmargins for SVG, Canvas, and native rendering.\n\n## Named scales and multiple axes\n\nThe reserved `x` and `y` entries are the default bindings for Cartesian marks.\nAdd another entry when a mark needs an independent mapping, then bind that mark\nwith `xScale` or `yScale`:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(revenue, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    lineY(conversion, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'rate',\n      yScale: 'conversion',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n    },\n    conversion: {\n      channel: 'y',\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      side: 'right',\n      axis: {\n        label: 'Conversion',\n        ticks: { format: (value: number) => `${Math.round(value * 100)}%` },\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=70-composed-chart height=480 -->\n\nA named scale must declare `channel: 'x'` or `channel: 'y'`. Its mark binding\nmust use the same channel. The ID `color` is reserved for the shared visual\ncolor scale and cannot name a Cartesian position scale.\n\nEvery non-null scale renders an axis by default. Set `axis: false` when a\nmapping should not draw another axis. X scales can use the top or bottom side,\nand y scales can use the left or right side. Axes on the same side stack\noutward and contribute their measured size to the automatic margin.\n\nAxis sides stay physical in a right-to-left container, so `side: 'right'`\nstill puts the axis on the right. Automatic tick anchors account for the\ncontainer's inline direction and keep labels outside the plot. An explicitly\nauthored `start` or `end` anchor remains logical. Text measurement, SVG,\nCanvas, native rendering, and standalone SVG export preserve that direction.\n\n## Automatic guide layout\n\nWhen a margin side is omitted, the scene compiler measures:\n\n- formatted tick glyph bounds\n- tick rotation\n- first and last label overhang\n- axis title bounds and offset\n- Cartesian `text`-mark anchors, pixel offsets, and rotation\n- color legend height\n\nThe DOM host measures the inherited container font and relayouts after web\nfonts load. Static compilation uses deterministic estimates unless\n`measureText` is supplied. Explicit margin sides remain locked, and\n`clip: true` prevents clipped mark labels from expanding the plot.\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartTextMeasurer {\n  (\n    text: string,\n    options: {\n      fontSize: number\n      fontWeight?: number\n      fontFamily: string\n      fontStyle: string\n      fontStretch: string\n      letterSpacing: number\n      direction: 'ltr' | 'rtl' | 'inherit'\n      locale?: string\n      fontScale: number\n      anchor: 'start' | 'middle' | 'end'\n      baseline: 'auto' | 'middle' | 'hanging'\n    },\n  ): {\n    x: number\n    y: number\n    width: number\n    height: number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe returned `x` and `y` locate the painted glyph box relative to the requested\nanchor and baseline. Pass the measurer through a host, adapter, runtime render,\nor `createChartScene` layout options. The callback is synchronous; a host owns\nfont loading or asynchronous native measurement and recompiles the scene when\nthose metrics become ready. `fontScale` applies to the painted font size and\nletter spacing, so custom measurers must include it in their result.\n\n## Custom scales\n\n`ChartScale` is the final extension boundary for a nonstandard positional\nmapping. Prefer a compact scale, then a D3 scale with the required semantics,\nbefore implementing this contract:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartScale {\n  id: string\n  resolve(context: ChartScaleResolveContext): ResolvedScale\n}\n\ntype ChartScaleResolver = (context: ChartScaleResolveContext) => ResolvedScale\n```\n\nThe resolver context contains `id`, all materialized `values`, the responsive\n`range`, the axis `options`, a target `tickCount`, and `includeZero`. It must\nreturn a complete `ResolvedScale`:\n\n```ts\ninterface ResolvedScale {\n  id: string\n  type: string\n  domain: readonly ChartValue[]\n  map(value: unknown): number\n  invert?(position: number): ChartValue\n  ticks: readonly { value: ChartValue; label: string; position: number }[]\n  bandwidth: number\n  viewport?: {\n    contentDomain: readonly ChartValue[]\n    domain: ChartContinuousDomain\n    translate: number\n    map(value: unknown): number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nPrefer a compact or D3 scale when it can express the mapping. A custom scale\nowns correct domains, finite mapping, ticks, formatting, bandwidth, and\nresponse to the supplied range. Do not wrap an existing compact or D3 scale in\n`ChartScale`.\n\nThe host cannot apply an authored `axis.viewport` to an opaque custom\n`ChartScale`. A custom resolver can return a complete\n`ResolvedScale.viewport` itself, in which case it owns the content domain,\ncommitted domain, translation, and presented mapper.\n\n## Color\n\nData-paint marks accept a semantic `color` channel. On marks that also expose\n`z`, grouping remains independent and supplies the color value only when\n`color` is omitted. `fill` and `stroke` are final paint overrides, so they do\nnot contribute to the scale or legend.\n\nOmit `color.scale` to use the theme palette. Use the compact ordinal scale when\ncategories need a stable application-owned mapping:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst statusColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['healthy', 'warning', 'critical'],\n  ['#16a34a', '#f59e0b', '#dc2626'],\n)\n```\n\nUpgrade color to D3 for continuous interpolation or quantile, quantize, and\nthreshold policy.\n\n```ts\ninterface ConfiguredColorScaleLike<TValue extends ChartKey, TOutput> {\n  (value: TValue): TOutput\n  copy: () => ConfiguredColorScaleLike<TValue, TOutput>\n  domain?: () => readonly TValue[]\n  range?: () => readonly TOutput[]\n}\n\ninterface ChartColorOptions {\n  scale?: ConfiguredColorScaleLike<any, any> | ChartColorScaleFactory<any, any>\n  resolver?: ChartColorScale\n  domain?: readonly ChartKey[]\n  range?: readonly string[]\n  nice?: boolean | number\n  legend?: ChartColorLegend\n}\n```\n\n| Option     | Default                 | Meaning                                                           |\n| ---------- | ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `scale`    | None                    | Compatible factory with inference or instance with a fixed domain |\n| `resolver` | None                    | Custom color-scale resolver                                       |\n| `domain`   | Observed channel values | Domain hint for factory, built-in, or custom resolution           |\n| `range`    | See below               | Range for a factory, the built-in scale, or a custom resolver     |\n| `nice`     | `false`                 | Nice a factory or configured continuous color scale               |\n| `legend`   | None                    | Legend layout and scene renderer shown above the inner chart      |\n\nResolution order:\n\n1. `color.scale`, created or copied before use\n2. custom `color.resolver`\n3. the built-in ordinal scale using `domain` or observed channel values and\n   `range` or the theme palette\n\nA color-scale factory is classified by its capabilities:\n\n- ordinal factories infer first-seen distinct values;\n- continuous and quantize factories infer the finite extent;\n- quantile factories receive the complete numeric population;\n- threshold factories require an explicit domain of authored cuts.\n\nThe theme palette is the range default only for the built-in ordinal scale.\nA D3 factory must receive `color.range` or return a scale already configured\nwith a non-empty string range or interpolator; bare D3 factories retain\nnumeric or empty defaults that are not valid paint. Multi-stop continuous\nranges receive evenly spaced domain stops across the extent. A supplied scale\ninstance retains its domain and range. Outputs are converted to strings. A\ncustom `ChartColorScale` receives observed values, optional domain and range,\nand the resolved theme, then returns:\n\n```ts\ninterface ResolvedColorScale {\n  type: string\n  kind?: 'categorical' | 'continuous' | 'quantile' | 'quantize' | 'threshold'\n  domain: readonly ChartKey[]\n  range: readonly string[]\n  thresholds?: readonly number[]\n  map(value: ChartKey | null | undefined): string\n}\n```\n\n`ChartKey` is `string | number`. On the built-in and configured-scale paths, a\nnull color value maps to the first range color or `currentColor`. A custom\n`ChartColorScale` owns its null mapping through `ResolvedColorScale.map`. A\ncustom stepped scale supplies exact interior legend boundaries with\n`thresholds`; D3 quantile, quantize, and threshold scales derive them\nautomatically.\n\n## Automatic color legend\n\n```ts\nimport { colorLegend, colorLegendItems } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  label: 'Package',\n  itemWidth: 120,\n  items: colorLegendItems({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 16,\n    indicator: { shape: 'square' },\n    label: { fontSize: 12 },\n  }),\n  width: 240,\n  format: (value) => value.toFixed(0),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\ninterface ColorLegendOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  label?: string\n  itemWidth?: number\n  items?: ColorLegendItems<TValue>\n  width?: number\n  format?: (value: number) => string\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n}\n\ndeclare function colorLegendItems<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey>(\n  options?: ColorLegendItemOptions<TValue>,\n): ColorLegendItems<TValue>\n\n// Opaque presentation returned by colorLegendItems().\ninterface ColorLegendItems<in TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {}\n\ninterface ColorLegendItemContext {\n  color: string\n  index: number\n  label: string\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendIndicatorRenderContext extends ColorLegendItemContext {\n  bounds: ChartBounds\n}\n\ntype ColorLegendItemValue<TValue extends ChartKey, TResult> =\n  TResult | ((value: TValue, context: ColorLegendItemContext) => TResult)\n\ntype ColorLegendIndicatorShape = 'dot' | 'square' | 'line' | 'line-dot'\n\ninterface ColorLegendItemOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  justify?: 'start' | 'center' | 'stretch'\n  gap?: number\n  rowGap?: number\n  indicator?: ColorLegendIndicatorOptions<TValue>\n  label?: ColorLegendLabelOptions<TValue>\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendIndicatorOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  shape?: ColorLegendItemValue<TValue, ColorLegendIndicatorShape>\n  width?: number\n  height?: number\n  gap?: number\n  render?: (\n    value: TValue,\n    context: ColorLegendIndicatorRenderContext,\n  ) => SceneNode | readonly SceneNode[]\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendLabelOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  format?: (value: TValue) => string\n  fontSize?: number\n  fontWeight?: number\n  fill?: ColorLegendItemValue<TValue, string>\n  fillOpacity?: number\n}\n```\n\n`items` applies only to categorical scales. The default `stretch`\njustification uses equal-width columns. In this mode, `itemWidth` defaults to\n`110` and is clamped to a minimum of `64`. `start` and `center` instead measure\neach formatted label with the chart host's configured typography, wrap compact\nrows, and use `gap` between items. Invalid host measurements fall back to the\nsame deterministic estimator used by chart layout.\n\nConfigured items default to a `16` pixel compact-layout gap, an `8` pixel row\ngap, an `8` by `8` dot with a `5` pixel label gap, `11` pixel label text, and\n`String` label formatting. Measured label height reserves enough room for each\nrow. If an indicator is wider than its allocated item, its bounds stop at the\nitem edge.\n\n`indicator.shape` selects a dot, square, line, or line with an outlined point.\n`width`, `height`, and `gap` reserve the indicator box and its distance from\nthe label. A custom `render` callback receives the categorical value plus\n`{ bounds, color, index, label }`; the built-in legend still measures, wraps,\nand positions the item. Label `fill` callbacks receive the same resolved color\nand formatted label. A line-dot center uses the chart background, or the host's\n`Canvas` color when that background is transparent, so it remains hollow on\nlight and dark hosts.\n\nAn item presentation can handle the same value type as its legend or a broader\none. TypeScript rejects a narrower presentation because its callbacks could\notherwise receive a value they do not accept.\n\nContinuous scales render a sampled ramp. Quantize, quantile, and threshold\nscales render exact range bins at their resolved thresholds. `width` and the\ntop-level `format` configure these quantitative forms, and `items` is ignored.\n`placement` defaults to `top`.\n\nThis mixed-series legend uses only the resolved color scale for labels and\npaint:\n\n```ts\ntype Series = 'Revenue' | 'Orders'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  items: colorLegendItems<Series>({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 20,\n    rowGap: 10,\n    indicator: {\n      width: 20,\n      height: 14,\n      gap: 6,\n      shape: (series) => (series === 'Revenue' ? 'line-dot' : 'square'),\n    },\n    label: {\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fill: (_series, { color }) => color,\n    },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n## Gradient legend\n\n```ts\nimport { colorGradientLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ncolorGradientLegend({\n  label: 'Density',\n  steps: 48,\n  width: 240,\n  format: (value) => value.toFixed(1),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\ninterface ColorGradientLegendOptions {\n  label?: string\n  steps?: number\n  width?: number\n  format?: (value: number) => string\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n}\n```\n\n`steps` defaults to `32` and is clamped to at least `2`. `width` defaults to\n`240`, uses at least `80` when space permits, and is finally capped by the\ninner chart width. The legend requires a numeric first and last color-domain\nvalue and throws for a nonnumeric domain.\n\n## Interactive categorical legend\n\n```ts\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ninteractiveColorLegend({\n  visible: controlledSignal(visibleSeries, setVisibleSeries),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  ariaLabel: 'Series visibility',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\ninterface InteractiveColorLegendChange<TValue extends ChartKey> {\n  type: 'toggle'\n  value: TValue\n  visible: boolean\n}\n\ninterface InteractiveColorLegendItemContext {\n  visible: boolean\n}\n\ninterface InteractiveColorLegendOptions<TValue extends ChartKey> {\n  hover?: 'series' | false\n  visible: ControlledSignal<\n    readonly TValue[],\n    InteractiveColorLegendChange<TValue>\n  >\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n  ariaLabel?: string\n  itemWidth?: number\n  format?: (value: TValue) => string\n  itemAriaLabel?: (\n    value: TValue,\n    context: InteractiveColorLegendItemContext,\n  ) => string\n  emptyLabel?: string\n}\n```\n\nThe application owns the current visible-value snapshot and handles each\nproposed replacement. The legend owns domain-ordered toggling, responsive\nlayout, and native browser buttons. It filters series geometry and focus points\nafter scale resolution, so hidden values remain in categorical and positional\ndomains. A mark participates when its categorical `color` channel establishes\nseries identity without a separate `z` channel. Dot marks also participate when\n`z` supplies their series and default color, with no separate `color` channel.\n\nThe DOM hosts replace the scene fallback with native `button` elements. Static\nSVG output retains a noninteractive visual fallback. This control is not yet\nimplemented by the React Native host.\n\nSet `hover: 'series'` to match inline mark states against the series under the\npointer or on the focused legend button. Its state context has `source:\n'legend'`, and `matches('series')` identifies the emphasized category. Leaving\nor blurring restores the chart's interaction styling. Hidden series do not\nreceive emphasis.\n\nEmphasis matches semantic series, not rendered color strings. Line and area\nmarks can establish a series through categorical `color`. For dot marks, use\n`z: 'series'` to establish series ownership; `z` also supplies the default\ncolor channel, so a second `color: 'series'` is unnecessary. A color-only dot\ncan vary its paint while remaining outside any semantic series.\n\nLegend emphasis does not set interaction focus, show a tooltip, move a\ncrosshair, or activate `whenFocused` geometry. Existing pinned focus and its\ntooltip stay in place. Tick-label opacity still sees the real interaction\nfocus. Static SVG remains noninteractive. A custom renderer must declare\n`supportsStateFocus: true` and honor the fourth `paintFocus` argument's\n`stateFocus`, otherwise requesting emphasis throws.\n\n## Custom legends\n\n`ChartColorLegend` separates layout from rendering:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartColorLegend {\n  height(itemCount: number, context: ChartColorLegendContext): number\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n  render(context: ChartColorLegendContext): SceneNode\n}\n```\n\n`height` returns the reserved pixel height before chart bounds are finalized.\nBoth callbacks receive the resolved colors, plot and legend bounds, theme, and\nfull chart size. `render` returns one keyed\n[scene node](./runtime-and-scene.md#scene-nodes). Browser host controls are an\nadvanced extension boundary used by `interactiveColorLegend`; ordinary custom\nlegends should remain renderer-neutral scene output.\n\n## Theme and gradients\n\nThe built-in theme is described in [Chart spec](./chart-spec.md#theme).\nChart gradients are renderer-neutral resources:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartGradientBase {\n  id: string\n  stops: readonly ChartGradientStop[]\n}\n\ninterface ChartGradientStop {\n  offset: number\n  color: string\n  opacity?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartLinearGradient extends ChartGradientBase {\n  type?: 'linear'\n  x1?: number\n  y1?: number\n  x2?: number\n  y2?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartRadialGradient extends ChartGradientBase {\n  type: 'radial'\n  cx?: number\n  cy?: number\n  r?: number\n  fx?: number\n  fy?: number\n}\n\ntype ChartGradient = ChartLinearGradient | ChartRadialGradient\n```\n\nCoordinates and stop offsets use SVG-style `objectBoundingBox` values\nnormalized from `0` to `1`. Renderers clamp values to that range. Stops keep\ntheir authored order, and an offset below the previous stop is clamped forward\nto the previous offset. A linear gradient may omit `type` for compatibility.\nIts omitted coordinates form a vertical gradient from bottom to top, from\n`(0, 1)` to `(0, 0)`. A radial gradient requires `type: 'radial'`; `cx`, `cy`,\nand `r` default to `0.5`, while omitted `fx` and `fy` independently inherit the\nresolved center coordinate.\n\nReference either kind from a mark's `fill` or `stroke` as `url(#resource-id)`.\nSVG and React Native support both kinds for fills and strokes. Canvas renders\nexact `objectBoundingBox` radial fills, including the elliptical result on a\nnon-square shape. Canvas radial strokes are not supported because a nonuniform\nbounds transform would also distort stroke width; the Canvas renderer throws\ninstead of painting a misleading approximation. Linear gradients continue to\nsupport Canvas fills and strokes.\n\n`ChartGradient`, `ChartGradientBase`, `ChartGradientStop`,\n`ChartLinearGradient`, and `ChartRadialGradient` are type exports from\n`@tanstack/charts`, `@tanstack/charts/universal`, and\n`@tanstack/charts/types`. Use an `idPrefix` when charts share a document; see\n[Rendering and export](./rendering-and-export.md#svg-resources).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Scales, Guides, and Color\ndescription: Reference for injected positional scales, automatic axes and margins, color scales, legends, themes, and gradients.\n---\n\nPass a compatible scale factory when its domain should come from mark channels.\nPass a scale instance when the domain is fixed application state. TanStack\nCharts copies the resolved scale, assigns its responsive pixel range, and uses\nthat copy for marks, ticks, and interaction. A supplied instance is never\nmutated.\n\nPut positional mappings in the chart's `scales` registry. The reserved `x`\nand `y` entries are the default bindings for Cartesian marks:\n\n```ts\nconst scales = {\n  x: { scale: scaleBand, axis: { label: 'Product' } },\n  y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Revenue' } },\n}\n```\n\nStart with the exact compact scale entry for numeric linear, band, point, or\nordinal mappings. Upgrade one mapping to D3 only when it needs temporal,\nnonlinear, radial, interpolated, or statistical scale semantics. The\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) guide owns that decision and direct\ndependency guidance. This page documents the TanStack Charts contract around\nboth implementations.\n\n## Default compact scale entries\n\n`@tanstack/charts` supplies four exact, tree-shakeable scale entries:\n\n| Entry                             | Runtime export | Type contract  |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------- |\n| `@tanstack/charts/scales/linear`  | `scaleLinear`  | `LinearScale`  |\n| `@tanstack/charts/scales/band`    | `scaleBand`    | `BandScale`    |\n| `@tanstack/charts/scales/point`   | `scalePoint`   | `PointScale`   |\n| `@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal` | `scaleOrdinal` | `OrdinalScale` |\n\n`LinearScale` supports numeric two-stop domains and ranges, `invert`, `clamp`,\n`ticks`, basic `tickFormat`, `nice`, and `copy`. `BandScale` supports\n`padding`, `paddingInner`, `paddingOuter`, `align`, `round`, `rangeRound`,\n`bandwidth`, `step`, and `copy`. `PointScale` exposes the corresponding point\noperations with zero bandwidth. `OrdinalScale` supports explicit or implicit\ndomains, cyclic ranges, `unknown`, and `copy`.\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]),\n}\n```\n\nThe factories and returned instances satisfy `ChartScaleInput` directly. They\nare a documented subset, not a complete `d3-scale` compatibility claim. Use D3\nfor temporal or transformed domains, piecewise and nonnumeric interpolation,\nand full D3 formatting semantics.\n\n`ConfiguredScaleLike` may expose `invert(position)`. Charts copies that\ncapability onto `ResolvedScale.invert` after assigning the responsive range.\nFinal-screen layouts and interactions can then recover semantic values without\ncopying the authored scale. Band scales do not expose inversion.\n\n## Positional scale factories\n\nThe common path passes a compact factory directly:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.16),\n  axis: { label: 'Product' },\n}\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  nice: true,\n  grid: true,\n  axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n}\n```\n\nThe chart creates a fresh scale for each layout, derives its domain from every\nmaterialized channel bound to that axis, applies `nice`, and assigns the\nresponsive range. Bar and area baselines contribute zero when their baseline\nis implicit. Empty channels retain the factory's native domain.\n\nReturn a configured scale from a zero-argument factory for options that should\nbe applied before domain inference:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.2),\n}\n```\n\n`nice` is an axis option because it must run after the inferred domain exists.\n\n## D3 scale upgrades\n\nCompact and D3 scales can coexist in one chart. This definition upgrades only\nits temporal x mapping:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true }\nconst y = { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true }\n```\n\nUse `scaleTime` or `scaleUtc` when dates need elapsed-time spacing or\ncalendar-aware ticks. Compact band and point scales accept `Date` categories,\nbut adjacent categories remain equally spaced regardless of the time between\nthem.\n\nOther D3-only scale families cover logarithmic, power, symlog, square-root,\nradial, sequential, diverging, quantile, quantize, and threshold mappings. D3\nlinear scales add piecewise domains and ranges, nonnumeric interpolation, and\ncustom interpolators. The factory-versus-instance and responsive-range rules\nremain unchanged after an upgrade.\n\n## Fixed domains\n\nPass a scale instance when the domain is semantic application state:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]),\n}\n```\n\nInstances retain:\n\n- their semantic domain\n- continuous, temporal, logarithmic, ordinal, or band mapping behavior\n- tick generation and default tick formatting\n- clamping, unknown values, interpolation, and padding configured by the caller\n\nTanStack Charts owns:\n\n- factory-domain inference\n- the responsive pixel range\n- y-range orientation and optional axis reversal\n- centering values within a band\n- guide placement, label measurement, and margins\n\nScale copies make one configured scale safe to reuse across responsive scenes.\n\n## Axis options\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartPositionScaleOptions<\n  TValue extends ChartValue,\n> extends ChartAxisOptions<TValue> {\n  channel?: 'x' | 'y'\n  side?: 'top' | 'right' | 'bottom' | 'left'\n}\n\ninterface ChartGuideLineStyle {\n  stroke?: string\n  strokeOpacity?: number\n  strokeWidth?: number\n  strokeDasharray?: string\n  lineCap?: 'butt' | 'round' | 'square'\n}\n\ninterface ChartAxisOptions<TValue extends ChartValue> {\n  scale: ChartScale | ChartScaleInput<TValue>\n  nice?: boolean | number\n  reverse?: boolean\n  viewport?: ChartAxisViewportOptions<Extract<TValue, ChartContinuousValue>>\n  grid?: boolean | ChartGuideLineStyle\n  axis?:\n    | false\n    | {\n        line?: boolean | ChartGuideLineStyle\n        ticks?:\n          | false\n          | {\n              count?: number\n              spacing?: number\n              values?: readonly TValue[]\n              size?: number\n              line?: false | ChartGuideLineStyle\n              padding?: number\n              format?: (value: TValue) => string\n            }\n        tickLabels?:\n          | false\n          | {\n              rotate?: number\n              fontSize?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              fontWeight?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              opacity?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelOpacityContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              anchor?:\n                | 'start'\n                | 'middle'\n                | 'end'\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => 'start' | 'middle' | 'end' | undefined)\n              dx?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              dy?:\n                | number\n                | ((\n                    context: ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue>,\n                  ) => number | undefined)\n              thin?:\n                | boolean\n                | {\n                    minGap?: number\n                    priority?: 'ends'\n                    keep?: readonly TValue[]\n                  }\n            }\n        label?:\n          | string\n          | {\n              text: string\n              offset?: number | 'auto'\n              fontSize?: number\n              fontWeight?: number\n              fill?: string\n              opacity?: number\n              motion?: ChartMotionDefinition\n            }\n      }\n}\n```\n\n| Option     | Default                      | Meaning                                                                  |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `scale`    | Required                     | Compact or D3 factory, configured instance, or custom `ChartScale`.      |\n| `nice`     | `false`                      | Nice the resolved domain using the responsive or supplied tick count.    |\n| `reverse`  | `false`                      | Reverses the responsive pixel range without changing the caller's scale. |\n| `viewport` | None                         | Commits a continuous semantic window and optional transient translation. |\n| `grid`     | `false`                      | Draws grid rules from semantic tick candidates.                          |\n| `axis`     | Inferred axis                | Axis line, tick candidates, labels, and title; `false` hides the axis.   |\n| `channel`  | Inferred for `x` and `y`     | Required on named scales; selects the Cartesian channel and range.       |\n| `side`     | `bottom` for x; `left` for y | Places an x axis on top/bottom or a y axis on left/right.                |\n\nSet `grid` or `axis.line` to a `ChartGuideLineStyle` object to enable the\nguide and override its stroke, opacity, width, dash pattern, or line cap.\nOmitted style fields keep the normal theme defaults. An empty object is\nequivalent to `true`, and `axis.line` styles only the axis baseline, not its\ntick stubs. Use finite non-negative widths and opacity values from zero to one.\n\n```ts\ntype ChartContinuousValue = number | Date\n\ntype ChartContinuousDomain<\n  TValue extends ChartContinuousValue = ChartContinuousValue,\n> =\n  | (Extract<TValue, number> extends never ? never : readonly [number, number])\n  | (Extract<TValue, Date> extends never ? never : readonly [Date, Date])\n\ninterface ChartAxisViewportOptions<\n  TValue extends ChartContinuousValue = ChartContinuousValue,\n> {\n  domain: ChartContinuousDomain<TValue>\n  translate?: number\n}\n```\n\n`ChartContinuousDomain` keeps numeric and temporal endpoints homogeneous at\nthe type boundary. At runtime, a viewport requires two distinct finite values\nand a configured or inferable continuous scale with `invert`, ticks, clamping\ndisabled, and independently configurable domain and range behavior. Band,\nordinal, quantize, clamped, and getter-only scale inputs are rejected. An\nauthored `axis.viewport` is also rejected for a custom `ChartScale`.\nLogarithmic content and viewport domains must be finite, nonzero, and stay on\nthe same side of zero.\n\n`translate` is applied in screen-direction scene pixels after semantic\nmapping: positive x moves right and positive y moves down, regardless of\ndomain order or `reverse`. Guides remain fixed. Viewport-dependent marks are\nclipped and translated per mark and per owned axis; see\n[Continuous viewports](../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md#continuous-viewports).\n\nSet `scales.x` or `scales.y` to `null` only when no mark uses that positional\nscale. To keep the scale while hiding its axis, use `axis: false`. Grid\nvisibility remains independent.\n\nWithout an explicit `axis.ticks` policy, the responsive target is\n`clamp(2, floor(chart.width / 92), 8)` for x and\n`clamp(2, floor(chart.height / 48), 7)` for y. The configured scale may return\na different number of ticks.\n\n`nice: true` uses that responsive or authored tick count when rounding a scale\ndomain, even if the axis is hidden. Use a number such as `nice: 5` when a small\nsparkline needs domain rounding independent of its size.\n\n`axis.ticks: false` removes both tick stubs and their candidate labels. To keep\nlabels while hiding only the stubs, use `axis: { ticks: { size: 0 } }`. The\nbaseline is separate, hide it with `axis.line: false`.\n\nUse `axis.ticks.line` to style the stubs with `ChartGuideLineStyle`, or set\nit to `false` to hide just the stubs. This option does not change the\nbaseline, grid, tick values, or label formatting.\n\n```ts\nconst axis = {\n  line: { stroke: 'navy' },\n  ticks: { line: { stroke: 'navy', strokeWidth: 2 } },\n}\n```\n\n`count`, `spacing`, and `values` are mutually exclusive candidate policies.\n`count` is a scale hint, `spacing` derives that hint from the final axis length,\nand `values` supplies exact semantic candidates. Grid lines and tick stubs use\nthese candidates before label thinning.\n\nTick labels are horizontal and collision-thinned by default. Rotation is\nexplicit and independent:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: scaleUtc,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: { spacing: 80, size: 0 },\n    tickLabels: {\n      rotate: -35,\n      thin: {\n        minGap: 8,\n        priority: 'ends',\n        keep: [launchDate],\n      },\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`thin: false` renders every candidate label. `keep` is a hard guarantee:\nkept values render even if they collide with one another. A kept value outside\nthe candidate set adds only a label, not a tick stub or grid line.\n\nTick-label presentation accepts constants or accessors. Accessors run on every\ncandidate before thinning and receive the semantic value, stable candidate\nindex, resolved center position, and resolved band width. Continuous scales\nreport a bandwidth of zero. Returning `undefined` preserves the normal value\nfor that candidate.\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartAxisTickLabelContext<TValue extends ChartValue> {\n  value: TValue\n  index: number\n  position: number\n  bandwidth: number\n}\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: scaleBand<number>().domain(weeks),\n  axis: {\n    tickLabels: {\n      fontSize: 13,\n      opacity: 0.62,\n      anchor: ({ index }) => (index === 0 ? 'start' : undefined),\n      dx: ({ index, bandwidth }) => (index === 0 ? -bandwidth / 2 : undefined),\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`anchor` defaults to an outward anchor derived from x rotation or the y-axis\nside. The automatic value accounts for the host's inline direction. `dx` and\n`dy` apply after the normal tick position and padding. Resolved font size,\nweight, anchor, offset, opacity, and rotation all participate in collision\nthinning and automatic margins. Numeric typography follows tick-label motion;\nanchor changes snap.\n\nAn opacity callback also receives `focus` and `pointer`, both `null` during\ninitial layout. It runs again when focus changes, updating paint without\nrebuilding marks, measuring text, or changing tick placement and thinning.\nClearing focus restores the initial opacity. Other tick-label callbacks remain\nlayout-only. For example, hide the tick that matches the focused x value:\n\n```ts\nconst tickLabels = {\n  opacity: ({ value, focus }) =>\n    focus && Object.is(value, focus.primary.xValue) ? 0 : 1,\n}\n```\n\nAxis titles keep the compact string form when only text is needed. Use the\nobject form for title typography, paint, offset, or motion:\n\n```ts\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  axis: {\n    label: {\n      text: 'Average order value (PLN)',\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fontWeight: 500,\n      fill: '#363636',\n      opacity: 0.9,\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`fill` sets the text color. Omitted presentation fields retain the existing\ntitle defaults: responsive 10 or 11 pixel sizing on x, 11 pixels on y, weight\n600, theme foreground fill, and 0.76 fill opacity. Configured font size and\nweight participate in text measurement, automatic offsets, and automatic\nmargins for SVG, Canvas, and native rendering.\n\n## Named scales and multiple axes\n\nThe reserved `x` and `y` entries are the default bindings for Cartesian marks.\nAdd another entry when a mark needs an independent mapping, then bind that mark\nwith `xScale` or `yScale`:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(revenue, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    lineY(conversion, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'rate',\n      yScale: 'conversion',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n    },\n    conversion: {\n      channel: 'y',\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      side: 'right',\n      axis: {\n        label: 'Conversion',\n        ticks: { format: (value: number) => `${Math.round(value * 100)}%` },\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=70-composed-chart height=480 -->\n\nA named scale must declare `channel: 'x'` or `channel: 'y'`. Its mark binding\nmust use the same channel. The ID `color` is reserved for the shared visual\ncolor scale and cannot name a Cartesian position scale.\n\nEvery non-null scale renders an axis by default. Set `axis: false` when a\nmapping should not draw another axis. X scales can use the top or bottom side,\nand y scales can use the left or right side. Axes on the same side stack\noutward and contribute their measured size to the automatic margin.\n\nAxis sides stay physical in a right-to-left container, so `side: 'right'`\nstill puts the axis on the right. Automatic tick anchors account for the\ncontainer's inline direction and keep labels outside the plot. An explicitly\nauthored `start` or `end` anchor remains logical. Text measurement, SVG,\nCanvas, native rendering, and standalone SVG export preserve that direction.\n\n## Automatic guide layout\n\nWhen a margin side is omitted, the scene compiler measures:\n\n- formatted tick glyph bounds\n- tick rotation\n- first and last label overhang\n- axis title bounds and offset\n- Cartesian `text`-mark anchors, pixel offsets, and rotation\n- color legend height\n\nThe DOM host measures the inherited container font and relayouts after web\nfonts load. Static compilation uses deterministic estimates unless\n`measureText` is supplied. Explicit margin sides remain locked, and\n`clip: true` prevents clipped mark labels from expanding the plot.\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartTextMeasurer {\n  (\n    text: string,\n    options: {\n      fontSize: number\n      fontWeight?: number\n      fontFamily: string\n      fontStyle: string\n      fontStretch: string\n      letterSpacing: number\n      direction: 'ltr' | 'rtl' | 'inherit'\n      locale?: string\n      fontScale: number\n      anchor: 'start' | 'middle' | 'end'\n      baseline: 'auto' | 'middle' | 'hanging'\n    },\n  ): {\n    x: number\n    y: number\n    width: number\n    height: number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe returned `x` and `y` locate the painted glyph box relative to the requested\nanchor and baseline. Pass the measurer through a host, adapter, runtime render,\nor `createChartScene` layout options. The callback is synchronous; a host owns\nfont loading or asynchronous native measurement and recompiles the scene when\nthose metrics become ready. `fontScale` applies to the painted font size and\nletter spacing, so custom measurers must include it in their result.\n\n## Custom scales\n\n`ChartScale` is the final extension boundary for a nonstandard positional\nmapping. Prefer a compact scale, then a D3 scale with the required semantics,\nbefore implementing this contract:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartScale {\n  id: string\n  resolve(context: ChartScaleResolveContext): ResolvedScale\n}\n\ntype ChartScaleResolver = (context: ChartScaleResolveContext) => ResolvedScale\n```\n\nThe resolver context contains `id`, all materialized `values`, the responsive\n`range`, the axis `options`, a target `tickCount`, and `includeZero`. It must\nreturn a complete `ResolvedScale`:\n\n```ts\ninterface ResolvedScale {\n  id: string\n  type: string\n  domain: readonly ChartValue[]\n  map(value: unknown): number\n  invert?(position: number): ChartValue\n  ticks: readonly { value: ChartValue; label: string; position: number }[]\n  bandwidth: number\n  viewport?: {\n    contentDomain: readonly ChartValue[]\n    domain: ChartContinuousDomain\n    translate: number\n    map(value: unknown): number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nPrefer a compact or D3 scale when it can express the mapping. A custom scale\nowns correct domains, finite mapping, ticks, formatting, bandwidth, and\nresponse to the supplied range. Do not wrap an existing compact or D3 scale in\n`ChartScale`.\n\nThe host cannot apply an authored `axis.viewport` to an opaque custom\n`ChartScale`. A custom resolver can return a complete\n`ResolvedScale.viewport` itself, in which case it owns the content domain,\ncommitted domain, translation, and presented mapper.\n\n## Color\n\nData-paint marks accept a semantic `color` channel. On marks that also expose\n`z`, grouping remains independent and supplies the color value only when\n`color` is omitted. `fill` and `stroke` are final paint overrides, so they do\nnot contribute to the scale or legend.\n\nOmit `color.scale` to use the theme palette. Use the compact ordinal scale when\ncategories need a stable application-owned mapping:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst statusColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['healthy', 'warning', 'critical'],\n  ['#16a34a', '#f59e0b', '#dc2626'],\n)\n```\n\nUpgrade color to D3 for continuous interpolation or quantile, quantize, and\nthreshold policy.\n\n```ts\ninterface ConfiguredColorScaleLike<TValue extends ChartKey, TOutput> {\n  (value: TValue): TOutput\n  copy: () => ConfiguredColorScaleLike<TValue, TOutput>\n  domain?: () => readonly TValue[]\n  range?: () => readonly TOutput[]\n}\n\ninterface ChartColorOptions {\n  scale?: ConfiguredColorScaleLike<any, any> | ChartColorScaleFactory<any, any>\n  resolver?: ChartColorScale\n  domain?: readonly ChartKey[]\n  range?: readonly string[]\n  nice?: boolean | number\n  legend?: ChartColorLegend\n}\n```\n\n| Option     | Default                 | Meaning                                                           |\n| ---------- | ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `scale`    | None                    | Compatible factory with inference or instance with a fixed domain |\n| `resolver` | None                    | Custom color-scale resolver                                       |\n| `domain`   | Observed channel values | Domain hint for factory, built-in, or custom resolution           |\n| `range`    | See below               | Range for a factory, the built-in scale, or a custom resolver     |\n| `nice`     | `false`                 | Nice a factory or configured continuous color scale               |\n| `legend`   | None                    | Legend layout and scene renderer shown above the inner chart      |\n\nResolution order:\n\n1. `color.scale`, created or copied before use\n2. custom `color.resolver`\n3. the built-in ordinal scale using `domain` or observed channel values and\n   `range` or the theme palette\n\nA color-scale factory is classified by its capabilities:\n\n- ordinal factories infer first-seen distinct values;\n- continuous and quantize factories infer the finite extent;\n- quantile factories receive the complete numeric population;\n- threshold factories require an explicit domain of authored cuts.\n\nThe theme palette is the range default only for the built-in ordinal scale.\nA D3 factory must receive `color.range` or return a scale already configured\nwith a non-empty string range or interpolator; bare D3 factories retain\nnumeric or empty defaults that are not valid paint. Multi-stop continuous\nranges receive evenly spaced domain stops across the extent. A supplied scale\ninstance retains its domain and range. Outputs are converted to strings. A\ncustom `ChartColorScale` receives observed values, optional domain and range,\nand the resolved theme, then returns:\n\n```ts\ninterface ResolvedColorScale {\n  type: string\n  kind?: 'categorical' | 'continuous' | 'quantile' | 'quantize' | 'threshold'\n  domain: readonly ChartKey[]\n  range: readonly string[]\n  thresholds?: readonly number[]\n  map(value: ChartKey | null | undefined): string\n}\n```\n\n`ChartKey` is `string | number`. On the built-in and configured-scale paths, a\nnull color value maps to the first range color or `currentColor`. A custom\n`ChartColorScale` owns its null mapping through `ResolvedColorScale.map`. A\ncustom stepped scale supplies exact interior legend boundaries with\n`thresholds`; D3 quantile, quantize, and threshold scales derive them\nautomatically.\n\n## Automatic color legend\n\n```ts\nimport { colorLegend, colorLegendItems } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  label: 'Package',\n  itemWidth: 120,\n  items: colorLegendItems({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 16,\n    indicator: { shape: 'square' },\n    label: { fontSize: 12 },\n  }),\n  width: 240,\n  format: (value) => value.toFixed(0),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\ninterface ColorLegendOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  label?: string\n  itemWidth?: number\n  items?: ColorLegendItems<TValue>\n  width?: number\n  format?: (value: number) => string\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n}\n\ndeclare function colorLegendItems<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey>(\n  options?: ColorLegendItemOptions<TValue>,\n): ColorLegendItems<TValue>\n\n// Opaque presentation returned by colorLegendItems().\ninterface ColorLegendItems<in TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {}\n\ninterface ColorLegendItemContext {\n  color: string\n  index: number\n  label: string\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendIndicatorRenderContext extends ColorLegendItemContext {\n  bounds: ChartBounds\n}\n\ntype ColorLegendItemValue<TValue extends ChartKey, TResult> =\n  TResult | ((value: TValue, context: ColorLegendItemContext) => TResult)\n\ntype ColorLegendIndicatorShape = 'dot' | 'square' | 'line' | 'line-dot'\n\ninterface ColorLegendItemOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  justify?: 'start' | 'center' | 'stretch'\n  gap?: number\n  rowGap?: number\n  indicator?: ColorLegendIndicatorOptions<TValue>\n  label?: ColorLegendLabelOptions<TValue>\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendIndicatorOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  shape?: ColorLegendItemValue<TValue, ColorLegendIndicatorShape>\n  width?: number\n  height?: number\n  gap?: number\n  render?: (\n    value: TValue,\n    context: ColorLegendIndicatorRenderContext,\n  ) => SceneNode | readonly SceneNode[]\n}\n\ninterface ColorLegendLabelOptions<TValue extends ChartKey = ChartKey> {\n  format?: (value: TValue) => string\n  fontSize?: number\n  fontWeight?: number\n  fill?: ColorLegendItemValue<TValue, string>\n  fillOpacity?: number\n}\n```\n\n`items` applies only to categorical scales. The default `stretch`\njustification uses equal-width columns. In this mode, `itemWidth` defaults to\n`110` and is clamped to a minimum of `64`. `start` and `center` instead measure\neach formatted label with the chart host's configured typography, wrap compact\nrows, and use `gap` between items. Invalid host measurements fall back to the\nsame deterministic estimator used by chart layout.\n\nConfigured items default to a `16` pixel compact-layout gap, an `8` pixel row\ngap, an `8` by `8` dot with a `5` pixel label gap, `11` pixel label text, and\n`String` label formatting. Measured label height reserves enough room for each\nrow. If an indicator is wider than its allocated item, its bounds stop at the\nitem edge.\n\n`indicator.shape` selects a dot, square, line, or line with an outlined point.\n`width`, `height`, and `gap` reserve the indicator box and its distance from\nthe label. A custom `render` callback receives the categorical value plus\n`{ bounds, color, index, label }`; the built-in legend still measures, wraps,\nand positions the item. Label `fill` callbacks receive the same resolved color\nand formatted label. A line-dot center uses the chart background, or the host's\n`Canvas` color when that background is transparent, so it remains hollow on\nlight and dark hosts.\n\nAn item presentation can handle the same value type as its legend or a broader\none. TypeScript rejects a narrower presentation because its callbacks could\notherwise receive a value they do not accept.\n\nContinuous scales render a sampled ramp. Quantize, quantile, and threshold\nscales render exact range bins at their resolved thresholds. `width` and the\ntop-level `format` configure these quantitative forms, and `items` is ignored.\n`placement` defaults to `top`.\n\nThis mixed-series legend uses only the resolved color scale for labels and\npaint:\n\n```ts\ntype Series = 'Revenue' | 'Orders'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  items: colorLegendItems<Series>({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 20,\n    rowGap: 10,\n    indicator: {\n      width: 20,\n      height: 14,\n      gap: 6,\n      shape: (series) => (series === 'Revenue' ? 'line-dot' : 'square'),\n    },\n    label: {\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fill: (_series, { color }) => color,\n    },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n## Gradient legend\n\n```ts\nimport { colorGradientLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ncolorGradientLegend({\n  label: 'Density',\n  steps: 48,\n  width: 240,\n  format: (value) => value.toFixed(1),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\ninterface ColorGradientLegendOptions {\n  label?: string\n  steps?: number\n  width?: number\n  format?: (value: number) => string\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n}\n```\n\n`steps` defaults to `32` and is clamped to at least `2`. `width` defaults to\n`240`, uses at least `80` when space permits, and is finally capped by the\ninner chart width. The legend requires a numeric first and last color-domain\nvalue and throws for a nonnumeric domain.\n\n## Interactive categorical legend\n\n```ts\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\ninteractiveColorLegend({\n  visible: controlledSignal(visibleSeries, setVisibleSeries),\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  ariaLabel: 'Series visibility',\n})\n```\n\n```ts\ninterface InteractiveColorLegendChange<TValue extends ChartKey> {\n  type: 'toggle'\n  value: TValue\n  visible: boolean\n}\n\ninterface InteractiveColorLegendItemContext {\n  visible: boolean\n}\n\ninterface InteractiveColorLegendOptions<TValue extends ChartKey> {\n  hover?: 'series' | false\n  visible: ControlledSignal<\n    readonly TValue[],\n    InteractiveColorLegendChange<TValue>\n  >\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n  ariaLabel?: string\n  itemWidth?: number\n  format?: (value: TValue) => string\n  itemAriaLabel?: (\n    value: TValue,\n    context: InteractiveColorLegendItemContext,\n  ) => string\n  emptyLabel?: string\n}\n```\n\nThe application owns the current visible-value snapshot and handles each\nproposed replacement. The legend owns domain-ordered toggling, responsive\nlayout, and native browser buttons. It filters series geometry and focus points\nafter scale resolution, so hidden values remain in categorical and positional\ndomains. A mark participates when its categorical `color` channel establishes\nseries identity without a separate `z` channel. Dot marks also participate when\n`z` supplies their series and default color, with no separate `color` channel.\n\nThe DOM hosts replace the scene fallback with native `button` elements. Static\nSVG output retains a noninteractive visual fallback. This control is not yet\nimplemented by the React Native host.\n\nSet `hover: 'series'` to match inline mark states against the series under the\npointer or on the focused legend button. Its state context has `source:\n'legend'`, and `matches('series')` identifies the emphasized category. Leaving\nor blurring restores the chart's interaction styling. Hidden series do not\nreceive emphasis.\n\nEmphasis matches semantic series, not rendered color strings. Line and area\nmarks can establish a series through categorical `color`. For dot marks, use\n`z: 'series'` to establish series ownership; `z` also supplies the default\ncolor channel, so a second `color: 'series'` is unnecessary. A color-only dot\ncan vary its paint while remaining outside any semantic series.\n\nLegend emphasis does not set interaction focus, show a tooltip, move a\ncrosshair, or activate `whenFocused` geometry. Existing pinned focus and its\ntooltip stay in place. Tick-label opacity still sees the real interaction\nfocus. Static SVG remains noninteractive. A custom renderer must declare\n`supportsStateFocus: true` and honor the fourth `paintFocus` argument's\n`stateFocus`, otherwise requesting emphasis throws.\n\n## Custom legends\n\n`ChartColorLegend` separates layout from rendering:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartColorLegend {\n  height(itemCount: number, context: ChartColorLegendContext): number\n  placement?: 'top' | 'bottom'\n  render(context: ChartColorLegendContext): SceneNode\n}\n```\n\n`height` returns the reserved pixel height before chart bounds are finalized.\nBoth callbacks receive the resolved colors, plot and legend bounds, theme, and\nfull chart size. `render` returns one keyed\n[scene node](./runtime-and-scene.md#scene-nodes). Browser host controls are an\nadvanced extension boundary used by `interactiveColorLegend`; ordinary custom\nlegends should remain renderer-neutral scene output.\n\n## Theme and gradients\n\nThe built-in theme is described in [Chart spec](./chart-spec.md#theme).\nChart gradients are renderer-neutral resources:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartGradientBase {\n  id: string\n  stops: readonly ChartGradientStop[]\n}\n\ninterface ChartGradientStop {\n  offset: number\n  color: string\n  opacity?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartLinearGradient extends ChartGradientBase {\n  type?: 'linear'\n  x1?: number\n  y1?: number\n  x2?: number\n  y2?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartRadialGradient extends ChartGradientBase {\n  type: 'radial'\n  cx?: number\n  cy?: number\n  r?: number\n  fx?: number\n  fy?: number\n}\n\ntype ChartGradient = ChartLinearGradient | ChartRadialGradient\n```\n\nCoordinates and stop offsets use SVG-style `objectBoundingBox` values\nnormalized from `0` to `1`. Renderers clamp values to that range. Stops keep\ntheir authored order, and an offset below the previous stop is clamped forward\nto the previous offset. A linear gradient may omit `type` for compatibility.\nIts omitted coordinates form a vertical gradient from bottom to top, from\n`(0, 1)` to `(0, 0)`. A radial gradient requires `type: 'radial'`; `cx`, `cy`,\nand `r` default to `0.5`, while omitted `fx` and `fy` independently inherit the\nresolved center coordinate.\n\nReference either kind from a mark's `fill` or `stroke` as `url(#resource-id)`.\nSVG and React Native support both kinds for fills and strokes. Canvas renders\nexact `objectBoundingBox` radial fills, including the elliptical result on a\nnon-square shape. Canvas radial strokes are not supported because a nonuniform\nbounds transform would also distort stroke width; the Canvas renderer throws\ninstead of painting a misleading approximation. Linear gradients continue to\nsupport Canvas fills and strokes.\n\n`ChartGradient`, `ChartGradientBase`, `ChartGradientStop`,\n`ChartLinearGradient`, and `ChartRadialGradient` are type exports from\n`@tanstack/charts`, `@tanstack/charts/universal`, and\n`@tanstack/charts/types`. Use an `idPrefix` when charts share a document; see\n[Rendering and export](./rendering-and-export.md#svg-resources).\n"}
